      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Resgo: Portfolio Simulation\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"End to end strategic value quantification\"\n",
        "author: \"Nicholas Dorsch\"\n",
        "date: today\n",
        "categories: [LDI, geology, geophysics, statistics]\n",
        "image: media/resgo_logo_white.svg\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  echo: false\n",
        "  cache: true\n",
        "---"
      ],
      "id": "dd243aa6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from datetime import datetime\n",
        "import altair as alt\n",
        "\n",
        "from ldstats import LogitNormal, LogNormal\n",
        "from resgo.model import (\n",
        "    DecisionRule,\n",
        "    EconomicModel,\n",
        "    FieldDevelopmentModel,\n",
        "    FlatPriceModel,\n",
        "    FlatRoyaltyModel,\n",
        "    FlatTaxModel,\n",
        "    Oil,\n",
        "    OilTypeCurve,\n",
        "    Portfolio,\n",
        "    Prospect,\n",
        "    ProspectNode,\n",
        "    Reservoir,\n",
        "    RiskDependency,\n",
        "    RiskDependencyProfile,\n",
        "    RiskProfile,\n",
        "    VolumetricModel,\n",
        "    Zone,\n",
        ")\n",
        "from resgo.plotting import PortfolioReporter, ProspectReporter\n",
        "\n",
        "from src.plotting import theme\n",
        "\n",
        "PLOTTING_BACKEND = \"altair\"\n",
        "\n",
        "CHAINS=8\n",
        "DRAWS=100\n",
        "SEED=42\n",
        "\n",
        "alt.data_transformers.disable_max_rows()\n",
        "theme.apply_lex_altair_theme()"
      ],
      "id": "d29cd73c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Resgo still has no UI, but before I start developing it I have one more feature to\n",
        "build: \n",
        "\n",
        "Portfolio simulation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Portfolio simulation is the Resgo end-game. Resgo already provides a very performant\n",
        "Prospect level simulation engine, from subsurface through to commercial metrics, and\n",
        "that leaves it well positioned for zooming out one more level to portfolio level\n",
        "analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "My goals at the outset are the following:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Allow grouping Prospects into Portfolios, with lossless representation\n",
        "2. Represent parent-child relationships between Prospects to allow for dependency\n",
        "   propagation\n",
        "3. Dependency propagation should allows information to flow between prospects and their\n",
        "   children, so that drilling outcomes follow logical chains during simulation\n",
        "4. Use those logical chains to derive stand-alone and full-cycle metrics for decision\n",
        "   gating\n",
        "5. Report accepted, strategic (full cycle economics says drill) and rejected prospect\n",
        "   decisions at the portfolio level\n",
        "6. Make results interpretable with well designed visualizations\n",
        "\n",
        "## Structure and constraints\n",
        "\n",
        "The most convenient representation of a Portfolio with these constraints in mind is a\n",
        "tree. Prospects can have children: opportunities who's risks are affected by outcomes in\n",
        "their parent. That creates some limitations (sibling Prospects can't have dependencies)\n",
        "but I think the simplicity of tree depth traversal, and limiting onerous dependency\n",
        "configuration, will make that trade-off worthwhile.\n",
        "\n",
        "For lossless Prospect representation in Resgo, dependency must be represented at the\n",
        "reservoir and risk factor level. This is a complication that I believe will pay\n",
        "dividends in granular configuration and interpretability of portfolio simulations,\n",
        "matching the mental models in subsurface teams' heads when defining dependencies between\n",
        "opportunitities.\n",
        "\n",
        "It will mean, for example, that reservoirs in the Propects tree can share strong\n",
        "P(Charge) dependency. If the parent fails on charge, that heavily impacts the P(Charge)\n",
        "of children. This is well suited for modelling play-openers on untested migration\n",
        "pathways, for example. Similarly these dependencies can be captured for reservoir\n",
        "presence for testing sand fairways, and seal/trap for things like demonstrating fault\n",
        "seal.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Dependency mechanics\n",
        "\n",
        "I'm going to bring up Bayes again, sorry[^not-sorry]. \n",
        "\n",
        "Dependency in this framing is a Bayesian probability update from the prior risk\n",
        "probability (say a 0.5 probability of charge in the child reservoir). Mechanically, the\n",
        "update is applied in odds space:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "O_{\\mathrm{post}} = O_{\\mathrm{prior}} \\times \\text{LH}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "or, equivalently, in log-odds space:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ln_2\\left(O_{\\mathrm{post}}\\right)\n",
        "= \\ln_2\\left(O_{\\mathrm{prior}}\\right) + \\ln_2\\left(\\text{LH}\\right)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $O = p / (1 - p)$ is the odds, $\\text{LH}$ is the likelihood ratio, and\n",
        "$\\ln_2$ denotes a base-2 logarithm. \n",
        "\n",
        "Using a base-2 logarithm means that the evidence term, $\\ln_2(\\text{LH})$, is measured\n",
        "in **bits**: positive bits increase the odds of success, negative bits decrease them,\n",
        "and zero bits leave the prior odds unchanged.\n",
        "\n",
        "You'll have to humor me---I know it seems weird---but this is a very mentally ergonomic\n",
        "way of framing Bayesian probability updates. \n",
        "\n",
        "Starting from a prior probability of 0.50 as an example, each additional bit doubles the\n",
        "likelihood ratio:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Evidence | Likelihood ratio | Posterior probability |\n",
        "|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| -4 bits | 1:16 | 0.06 |\n",
        "| -3 bits | 1:8 | 0.11 |\n",
        "| -2 bits | 1:4 | 0.20 |\n",
        "| -1 bit | 1:2 | 0.33 |\n",
        "| 0 bits | 1:1 | 0.50 |\n",
        "| 1 bit | 2:1 | 0.67 |\n",
        "| 2 bits | 4:1 | 0.80 |\n",
        "| 3 bits | 8:1 | 0.89 |\n",
        "| 4 bits | 16:1 | 0.94 |\n",
        "\n",
        "Operating in this way means that updates are commutative: you can add and subtract bits\n",
        "of evidence to arrive at a final posterior. This is mental Bayes trickery, but is also\n",
        "useful for efficient and interpretable communication of risk dependencies in the context\n",
        "of the portfolio simulator.\n",
        "\n",
        "> _\"If the Buffet prospect fails on charge, how bad would that be for charge risk at\n",
        "> Bonanza?\"_\n",
        ">\n",
        "> _\"They're on the same migration corridor so really bad... probably -4 bits.\"_\n",
        ">\n",
        "> _\"We did see shows on that separate migration corridor connecting to Bonanza though,\n",
        "> remember?\"_\n",
        ">\n",
        "> _\"Fair enough, that's probably worth 1 bit. -3 bits total then.\"_\n",
        "\n",
        "Now, regardless of P(Charge) at Buffet, the information it provides to Bonanza is\n",
        "explicitly communicated. \n",
        "\n",
        "If, for example, P(Charge) is 0.5 across prospects and Buffet fails on charge, that\n",
        "means Bonanza gets a P(Charge) of 0.11 (killing it). If Buffet _is_ charged though, that\n",
        "increase P(Charge) at Bonanza to 0.89!\n",
        "\n",
        "See? We can all become Bayesians with some language tricks.\n",
        "\n",
        "There is a technical wrinkle that positive and negative updates are not necessarily\n",
        "symmetric (relevant example\n",
        "[here](https://nick-dorsch.github.io/blog/posts/confusion/)), so the code should make\n",
        "that explicit, but default to symmetric updates.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Decision Gates\n",
        "\n",
        "For the portfolio simulation to be coherent, decisions need to be made about whether to\n",
        "drill for each Prospect, based on the information available. That requires point\n",
        "estimate hurdles. Decisions aren't made stochastically. We can't just use the simulation\n",
        "result to determine the right path on every iteration, because the decision maker\n",
        "doesn't know what will happen. That means a decision criterion needs to be specified. \n",
        "\n",
        "This can be things like a positive risked mean NPV (equivalent to EMV):\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\mathrm{NPV}_{\\mathrm{risked}}\\right] > 0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "or a positive risked 90th percentile NPV:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q_{0.10}\\left(\\mathrm{NPV}_{\\mathrm{risked}}\\right) > 0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "But it needs to be agreed upon up front for the portfolio simulation to operate. Most\n",
        "companies have defined hurdles like this in place, but there may be situations where\n",
        "hurdles are prospect specific.\n",
        "\n",
        "Luckily, because Resgo simulates everything in a single integrated chain, these decision\n",
        "metrics will be consistently applied to rigorously defined stochastic ranges of NPV. I\n",
        "would imagine that that is a rare luxury in portfolio analysis contexts.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Simulation process\n",
        "\n",
        "Resgo allows us to very ergonomically simulate the entire portfolio tree in a forward\n",
        "pass, adjusting risk factors on success/failure case of parent nodes, then run the\n",
        "decision gating logic on the results to determine which prospects get drilled and which\n",
        "get rejected. We can then aggregate the portfolio level NPV to assess its overall\n",
        "economic value and uncertainty.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Additional wrinkles\n",
        "\n",
        "Because we are simulating an ensemble of prospects, we need to be careful to define a\n",
        "few things up front. \n",
        "\n",
        "We need a single price deck, especially if it is stochastic, to avoid individual\n",
        "prospects getting their own price histories.\n",
        "\n",
        "We also need a single economic basis for escalation and discounting. That means every\n",
        "prospect has NPV calculations consistent on a single \"start date\" for the whole\n",
        "portfolio. Failing to do this would unfairly inflate the value of future prospects in\n",
        "the schedule.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Strategic value\n",
        "\n",
        "Something compelling that this gives us is the ability to quantify the strategic value\n",
        "of prospects. This is the stand-alone value of the prospect, plus any downstream\n",
        "opportunity it creates.\n",
        "\n",
        "This means that a stand-alone prospect with negative NPV---maybe due to a low\n",
        "P(Success)---can have its downstream value quantified and summed to make it _full-cycle_\n",
        "economic, even if it is not drillable on its own merits.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{mermaid}\n",
        "flowchart TD\n",
        "    Buffet[\"Buffet<br/>P(Success): 0.45<br/>NPV: $180MM\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Buffet --> Cove[\"Cove<br/>P(Success): 0.35<br/>NPV: $70MM\"]\n",
        "    Buffet --> Ledge[\"Ledge<br/>P(Success): 0.55<br/>NPV: $75MM\"]\n",
        "    Buffet --> Bridge[\"Bridge<br/>P(Success): 0.10<br/>NPV: -$200MM\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Bridge --> Bonanza[\"Bonanza<br/>P(Success): 0.15<br/>NPV: $1,200MM\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Crest[\"Crest<br/>P(Success): 0.20<br/>NPV: $700MM\"]\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Saddle[\"Saddle<br/>P(Success): 0.18<br/>NPV: $650MM\"]\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Spur[\"Spur<br/>P(Success): 0.12<br/>NPV: $1,000MM\"]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Hear me out.\n",
        "\n",
        "Bridge is a high risk, low return prospect. It will probably fail, and it's a small\n",
        "closure. It's a dog.\n",
        "\n",
        "But if Buffet were successful, you'd be crazy not to drill Bridge next.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the crazy senior geologist speaking. He has a mad twinkle in his eye, and he knows\n",
        "nobody will listen, but he knows he is _right, dammit_.\n",
        "\n",
        "Why is he right?\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the information that Bridge provides, expensive as it may be, has huge upside.\n",
        "\n",
        "A success at Bridge would boost the P(Success) of Bonanza, a high risk, high reward\n",
        "prospect that nobody is considering because \"there is no migration that way\". Bonanza is\n",
        "a _play opener_, it has several follow-ups, all of which are currently high risk, but\n",
        "high value.\n",
        "\n",
        "So you drill Bridge to open up the play.\n",
        "\n",
        "I've been the crazy guy before. I've never been able to quantify any of this stuff\n",
        "persuasively. Resgo lets me quantify the whole lot, all the way through to economics. It\n",
        "all flows naturally as a consequence of your decision metrics, and the risks and\n",
        "uncertainties in your portfolio. \n",
        "\n",
        "Resgo tells you to do it.\n",
        "\n",
        "And the tool isn't crazy! It's just uncertainty propagation, Bayesian updating and\n",
        "commercial modelling. Don't argue with the results, just make sure you verify your\n",
        "inputs.\n",
        "\n",
        "Now let's try to do it.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Here's the Portfolio tree again, without risks and NPVs as we haven't calculated them\n",
        "yet:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```{mermaid}\n",
        "flowchart TD\n",
        "    Buffet[Buffet]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Buffet --> Cove[Cove]\n",
        "    Buffet --> Ledge[Ledge]\n",
        "    Buffet --> Bridge[Bridge]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Bridge --> Bonanza[Bonanza]\n",
        "\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Crest[Crest]\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Saddle[Saddle]\n",
        "    Bonanza --> Spur[Spur]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "My goal with this example will be to live demo the sequence I spelled out in the\n",
        "previous section on strategic value.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is all make believe, but what I want to try and illustrate is that standalone\n",
        "prospect economics is insufficient for good opportunity ranking or decision making.\n",
        "These prospects will look far too risky to drill on their own, but at a portfolio level\n",
        "warrant serious consideration.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, let's define these prospects. All prospects will share the same set of\n",
        "reservoir targets, named \"Archer\", \"Boulder\", \"Canyon\" and \"Drift\".\n",
        "\n",
        "This will be a bit of a repetitive section, so feel free to look through one prospect,\n",
        "then skip ahead.\n"
      ],
      "id": "6eaed10e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Set up shared zone information\n",
        "\n",
        "zone_corr_matrix = [\n",
        "    [1.0, 0.20, -0.10],\n",
        "    [0.20, 1.0, -0.30],\n",
        "    [-0.10, -0.30, 1.0],\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "zones = {\n",
        "    \"Archer\": Zone(\n",
        "        name=\"Archer\",\n",
        "        net_m=LogNormal.elicit(8.0, 18.0),\n",
        "        porosity=LogitNormal.elicit(0.16, 0.24),\n",
        "        water_saturation=LogitNormal.elicit(0.28, 0.42),\n",
        "        corr_matrix=zone_corr_matrix,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Boulder\": Zone(\n",
        "        name=\"Boulder\",\n",
        "        net_m=LogNormal.elicit(12.0, 28.0),\n",
        "        porosity=LogitNormal.elicit(0.14, 0.22),\n",
        "        water_saturation=LogitNormal.elicit(0.30, 0.46),\n",
        "        corr_matrix=zone_corr_matrix,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Canyon\": Zone(\n",
        "        name=\"Canyon\",\n",
        "        net_m=LogNormal.elicit(6.0, 14.0),\n",
        "        porosity=LogitNormal.elicit(0.18, 0.27),\n",
        "        water_saturation=LogitNormal.elicit(0.24, 0.38),\n",
        "        corr_matrix=zone_corr_matrix,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Drift\": Zone(\n",
        "        name=\"Drift\",\n",
        "        net_m=LogNormal.elicit(20.0, 45.0),\n",
        "        porosity=LogitNormal.elicit(0.10, 0.18),\n",
        "        water_saturation=LogitNormal.elicit(0.34, 0.52),\n",
        "        corr_matrix=zone_corr_matrix,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "}"
      ],
      "id": "71c9cc9f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Set up risk profiles\n",
        "\n",
        "risk_profiles = {\n",
        "    \"Archer\": RiskProfile.from_probs(\n",
        "        p_reservoir=0.92,\n",
        "        p_seal=0.95,\n",
        "        p_closure=0.96,\n",
        "        p_charge=0.40,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Boulder\": RiskProfile.from_probs(\n",
        "        p_reservoir=0.88,\n",
        "        p_seal=0.93,\n",
        "        p_closure=0.97,\n",
        "        p_charge=0.40,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Canyon\": RiskProfile.from_probs(\n",
        "        p_reservoir=0.94,\n",
        "        p_seal=0.90,\n",
        "        p_closure=0.95,\n",
        "        p_charge=0.40,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"Drift\": RiskProfile.from_probs(\n",
        "        p_reservoir=0.86,\n",
        "        p_seal=0.92,\n",
        "        p_closure=0.94,\n",
        "        p_charge=0.40,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "}"
      ],
      "id": "9d1d3a44",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Prospects\n",
        "\n",
        "### Buffet\n",
        "\n",
        "Buffet is a risky but high upside prospect that is the overall play opener for the\n",
        "portfolio. The biggest concern the team has is with charge, as they are not convinced a\n",
        "migration pathway exists at this location. The trap size is also a big uncertainty,\n",
        "leading to wide uncertainty in outcomes.\n"
      ],
      "id": "fc859e64"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "\n",
        "buffet_area_km2 = (2.0, 10.0)\n",
        "\n",
        "buffet_reservoirs = [\n",
        "    Reservoir(\n",
        "        area_km2=LogNormal.elicit(*buffet_area_km2),\n",
        "        zone=zones[\"Archer\"],\n",
        "        oil=Oil.default(),\n",
        "        oil_type_curve=OilTypeCurve.default(),\n",
        "        risk_profile=risk_profiles[\"Archer\"],\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    Reservoir(\n",
        "        area_km2=LogNormal.elicit(*buffet_area_km2),\n",
        "        zone=zones[\"Boulder\"],\n",
        "        oil=Oil.default(),\n",
        "        oil_type_curve=OilTypeCurve.default(),\n",
        "        risk_profile=risk_profiles[\"Boulder\"],\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    Reservoir(\n",
        "        area_km2=LogNormal.elicit(*buffet_area_km2),\n",
        "        zone=zones[\"Canyon\"],\n",
        "        oil=Oil.default(),\n",
        "        oil_type_curve=OilTypeCurve.default(),\n",
        "        risk_profile=risk_profiles[\"Canyon\"],\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    Reservoir(\n",
        "        area_km2=LogNormal.elicit(*buffet_area_km2),\n",
        "        zone=zones[\"Drift\"],\n",
        "        oil=Oil.default(),\n",
        "        oil_type_curve=OilTypeCurve.default(),\n",
        "        risk_profile=risk_profiles[\"Drift\"],\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "buffet = Prospect(\n",
        "    name=\"Buffet\",\n",
        "    volumetric_model=VolumetricModel(\n",
        "        reservoirs=buffet_reservoirs,\n",
        "        dependency=0.5,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    field_development_model=FieldDevelopmentModel.simple_development(\n",
        "        num_producers=10,\n",
        "        reservoir_names=None,\n",
        "        start_date=datetime(2026, 1, 1),\n",
        "        month_offset=12,\n",
        "        drill_to_online_months=12,\n",
        "        max_oil_rate_stbpd=35_000,\n",
        "        producer_capex_USD=55e6,\n",
        "        years=30,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    economic_model=EconomicModel.from_values(\n",
        "        price_model=FlatPriceModel(oil_price_USD_stb=75.0, gas_price_USD_mmbtu=4.0),\n",
        "        royalty_model=FlatRoyaltyModel(royalty_rate=0.10),\n",
        "        tax_model=FlatTaxModel(tax_rate=0.30),\n",
        "        infrastructure_capex_USD=150e6,\n",
        "        annual_opex_USD=12e6,\n",
        "        producer_abandonment_cost_USD=2e6,\n",
        "        dry_hole_cost_USD=40e6,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "buffet_simpack = buffet.simulate(\n",
        "    risked=True,\n",
        "    levered=False,\n",
        "    chains=CHAINS,\n",
        "    draws=DRAWS,\n",
        "    seed=SEED,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "buffet_reporter = ProspectReporter(prospect=buffet, simpack=buffet_simpack)"
      ],
      "id": "05339c94",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
